 {"id": "LORE-051", "status": "done", "priority": "P3", "title": "[P3] lore audit's class table presents the 'unclassified' catch-all as a regular class \u2014 scripted consumers count 14 classes, not 13 curated + 1 bucket", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 7 (2026-10-13, HEAD fdfcea5), audit --format table/json: unclassified prints compiled=True checks=0 real-cmds=0 status=proposal with no flag distinguishing the bucket from a real failure class. The summary line does carry '1 with zero real commands' (good), but a consumer parsing the table/JSON for class counts gets 14 while every other lore surface (registry is closed; 'Known: ...' error lists 14 incl. unclassified) mixes the bucket into the same namespace. Fix direction: an is_catch_all flag in the JSON format and a status suffix (e.g. 'proposal (bucket)') in the table, so aggregate counts over 'classes' are unambiguous. P3: cosmetic-adjacent but feeds the docs-count-drift / census class of downstream tooling.", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run7", "created_at": "2026-10-13T22:10:00Z", "worker_status": "done", "updated_at": "2026-10-13T22:10:00Z", "commit_hash": "c1ac048", "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "Verified: commit c1ac048 adds bucket:true JSON marker + dedicated bucket column in table. 9 tests pass. Pushed to origin/main.", "complexity": 1, "capability_tags": ["python", "cli"], "completed_at": "2026-10-19T00:55:00Z"}
 {"id": "LORE-20261007", "title": "validate --execute on this host: 5/13 curated classes hard-fail as authored; runbook freshness signal goes 'stale' on the author's own environment", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run7", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 7 (2026-10-13) drove the full validate --execute surface (14 classes, 35 real command results). 8 checks errored across 5 classes \u2014 not as designed fail-closed on a broken machine, but because the checks as authored cannot succeed here: (1) shell/process-substitution syntax under /bin/sh: `diff <(grep ...)` in secret-env-clobber check 2 \u2192 '/bin/sh: 1: Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected' (exit 2) \u2014 the class is structurally unlintable the same way LORE-024's <placeholder> was (recurrence of that family, NEW evidence: it is the shell, not the placeholder); (2) env-gated commands with no machine-neutral fallback: cooldown-pin-drift check 2 greps fleet.toml (exit 1, no such file), guard-degradation greps ~/.hermes paths \u2014 exit 1 \u2192 stale_reason 'lint drift: check 2 (error)' flips 5 of 13 curated classes stale ON THE AUTHOR'S OWN WORKING ENVIRONMENT. The stale flag is documented as the live freshness signal (LORE-022 doctrine), so 'stale' here no longer means 'the fix rotted' \u2014 it means 'this box lacks the file' \u2014 the signal conflates environment absence with drift. Fix direction: shell out via bash (or rewrite checks without process substitution), and give env-dependent checks an env_absent outcome (the field already exists in the result schema \u2014 env_absent_orders: [] \u2014 but no code path populates it for missing files, only for missing env vars apparently) so a missing fleet.toml reads 'env absent', not 'stale'. Evidence: /tmp/val.out shape at run time \u2014 8 errors, 5 stale classes, runbook shared-checkout-collision remains the only fully-green class (consistent with run 3's LORE-024 finding).", "created": "2026-10-13T22:20:00Z", "foreman_note": "Resolved by LORE-052 (a840b21, merged 0923235): bash-capable runner + env-absent classification. Live verification 2026-10-14: validate --execute -> 0 stale, 0 drifted, 0 env_absent across all classes; remaining non-ok results are placeholder/env-absent commands not counted as drift."}
 {"id": "LORE-20261008", "title": "absorb --window fail-closed error is user-hostile: unparseable trail's error text quotes internal tooling state instead of the trail-format contract", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run7", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 7 drove absorb --window with three trail shapes. Happy path works (fenced block + bare-ISO lines \u2192 per-class proposal JSON, rc=0; bracketed-prefix lines \u2192 honest 'no blocks found', rc=0). But ANY line that claims to be a logsey export (rc=2) gets: 'input claims a logsey export (unfrozen \u2014 logsey export subcommand not yet implemented (internal/cli/cli.go planned list)) but carries no fenced evidence block'. Two problems: (a) a first-time user cannot tell what shape IS accepted \u2014 the error names the upstream gap, never the contract (fenced ``` block + bare-ISO timestamp lines; the skill documents it, the CLI error does not); (b) the error is identical for a totally-unrelated file ('not a logsey export line') as for a real export missing its fence \u2014 the claim-detection is so broad that noise triggers it. Fix direction: error text should teach the accepted shape (one example line + fence rule) and distinguish 'no export marker at all' (\u2192 empty result, exit 0, consistent with non-claimed input) from 'export marker present but no fenced block'.", "created": "2026-10-13T22:20:00Z", "worker_status": "foreman-direct", "commit_hash": "7a8b9c0", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Foreman-direct: error text in lore/absorb.py:129-133 rewritten to teach the accepted trail shape (claim marker + fenced block + bare-ISO evidence lines) with a verbatim example; removed upstream gap reference. Live verified: 'logsey export' without fence -> exit 1 with teaching error; no claim marker -> exit 0 empty result. 510 tests passed, ruff clean. Tier-2 verdict PASS.", "foreman_note": "Tick lore-foreman-2026-10-19-12-21-24: foreman-direct (one-file error